# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings.
#
# This exists because of a real, expensive failure: docker/backend/entrypoint.sh was checked out
# with CRLF on a Windows machine, so its shebang line read `#!/bin/sh\r`. Linux then looked for an
# interpreter literally named `/bin/sh\r`, did not find one, and reported
#
#     exec /usr/local/bin/entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory
#
# which names the SCRIPT while actually complaining about the INTERPRETER. The script was present,
# executable and correct. Every shell script that is executed inside a Linux container or on an
# EC2 instance must therefore be LF regardless of the platform it was edited on.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Let git decide what is text, and normalise it to LF in the repository.
* text=auto

# Anything a Linux kernel has to exec, or a container runtime has to parse, is LF ALWAYS — never
# translated to CRLF on checkout, even on Windows.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf

# Windows-only helpers keep CRLF; cmd.exe and PowerShell scripts are read by Windows.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touch, never diff as text.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.webp          binary
*.pdf           binary
*.docx          binary
*.xlsx          binary
*.zip           binary
*.jar           binary
*.keystore      binary
*.ttf           binary
*.otf           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.db            binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The vendored tracing engine stays LF everywhere, so the SHA-256 digests recorded in
# `frontend/lib/trace/UPSTREAM-MANIFEST.txt` mean the same thing on every machine. Under
# `* text=auto` above, a Windows checkout would rewrite the newlines and every digest in that
# manifest would mismatch on every Windows machine while the files were in fact correct.
#
# There used to be a second entry here, `-text` on `android/app/src/main/assets/dw-trace-engine.js`,
# for the same reason one level down: the handset ran this engine as a minified JavaScript bundle
# built from these sources, and a CRLF rewrite would have broken the byte-for-byte check on it. The
# handset now runs the engine as Kotlin — `android/core-imaging`, `core-vector`, `core-pipeline` and
# `core-export`, hashed in `android/UPSTREAM-MANIFEST-KOTLIN.txt` — so there is no generated asset
# left to protect. This line still earns its keep for the TypeScript the web traces with.
frontend/lib/trace/**  text eol=lf
